# These patterns were here all along and did nothing, because .gitignore has no
# effect on files git is already tracking. prod/app.env and test/app.env were
# committed before the rules existed, so they stayed tracked — and went public
# with the repo on 2026-07-30, carrying a Firebase service-account private key,
# a database password and an APP_KEY. Untracked on 2026-07-30.
#
# The files themselves are not source: pullenv.sh fetches them from
# s3://$ENV-tops-deploy/app/app.env, which is the source of truth. If you need
# them locally, pull them — never commit them back.
#
# If you add an env file here, confirm it is actually ignored:
#   git check-ignore -v <path>     # silent output means it is TRACKED
*.env
app.env
*/app.env
*/*/app.env